WEBVTT

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:12.630
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:17.750
 In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers,

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.420
 artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds. We'll hear about food and the experience

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:29.420
 of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions and tracing recipes

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:35.000
 through the movements of diaspora. Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.590
 Wills. You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:47.890
 And you can find all of our episodes along with pictures and more on the website deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:59.350
 Sana Javeri Kadri is a sometimes salty, permanently hungry, rather creative human. She was raised

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.070
 in post-colonial Bombay, wound up in the produce isles of California, and can be currently found

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:09.710
 at Sana Javeri Kadri on Instagram or in person wherever there are vegetables to be found.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:16.320
 She is also Schemer-in-Chief for Diaspora Co-op. In this episode, Sana talks with Chelsea

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.300
 about living between Bombay and the Bay Area, the role of aesthetics in telling difficult

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:26.300
 stories, and decolonization as a series of questions. A heads up, Sana and Chelsea drop

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:31.270
 a few swear words in this episode. Here's Chelsea speaking with Sana Javeri Kadri.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:41.620
 Welcome to the show. I'm so glad to be here. This is great. I already, like just in our like

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:47.180
 pre-conversation, I feel like you said all these things that made me recontextualize all the

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.860
 things I've been thinking about. So hopefully in an hour, we'll come out with more brilliance

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:59.220
 and I won't have to do anything. But my name is Sana Javeri Kadri. I'm a photographer most

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:07.820
 days for work. And then also an artist, most recently a businesswoman, question mark. And

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:14.690
 also work in food branding and marketing. And I think we're here to talk about moving around and

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.890
 migration and diaspora amongst other things. Yeah.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:24.390
 Yeah, that sounds right. So I started learning about your work through People's Kitchen Collective. And

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:29.270
 the joke this season is I'm doing a season about migration. And basically, that means I'm just

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:34.910
 interviewing People's Kitchen Collective on multiple continents. And why don't we start with,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.020
 what do you think your first memory of food is?

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:45.670
 This is actually a very clear memory. I remember our cook Lakshmi, who is still my family's cook,

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:52.530
 sneaking me chocolate. So I was sitting on the balcony of our apartment in Mumbai. And I was in

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.430
 this little yellow rocker. And I think I only remember it because it's now my brother's rocker.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.920
 And she came up to me and she knew that I hadn't been eating all day. And she gave me three pieces

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:09.320
 of chocolate. And I remember eating it super slowly. And then my mom walking in as I was on the last

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:15.970
 piece. And this is all a kind of foggy memory. But as I was on the last piece, she asked Lakshmi,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:20.000
 you know, what did you give her? And Lakshmi was like, nothing, just something for her to enjoy.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.520
 And they proceeded again to this huge fight in front of me. And I just remember looking

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:29.080
 back and forth between them and eating my piece of chocolate. And I think I was probably like three

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:36.830
 and a half. So it's a really early memory. But Lakshmi also has this running joke that she's been

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.870
 saying since I was born that nobody would ever kidnap me because I eat too much. They would send me

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.870
 back. So yeah.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.830
 Is it true? Do you eat too much?

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:51.180
 I definitely eat too much. I mean, Sakib and I bond about this all the time because we're

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:57.760
 every minute is spent thinking about food. And I mean, I definitely I've gone through periods when

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.850
 it's been unhealthy and I've struggled with it and been like, why are you so obsessed with food? And

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:07.850
 it's a method of control. But right now, I'm in a really good phase of being obsessed with food.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:15.970
 That's good. Where did you grow up? And Sakib was telling me as I was coming over here that,

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:19.280
 you know, your life has been in lots of different parts of the world.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:29.570
 Yeah, my parents were in Berkeley when they decided to have a family and they went from coming from very

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:37.110
 conventional Indian households of sort of middle class aspirations to coming together against their

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:43.380
 parents wishes and really wanting to like trailblaze their own life and create a family and a I guess

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.370
 career for themselves that wasn't what they came from. And I think I usually give my mom a lot of credit

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:53.950
 for it because the feminist in me wants to smash the patriarchy every day. But I think that for my dad

00:04:53.950 --> 00:05:01.970
 to coming from a very status oriented family where his last name holds a lot of weight, it was very brave

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:08.680
 and very hard to leave that for wanting to work in, you know, development and urban planning and

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:15.200
 architecture and wanting to change the world. What I'm curious about is the home has been a lot of

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.220
 different places for you. So where do you call home now? And I think I'm really interested in like,

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.120
 what does that taste like?

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:30.390
 So I think a year ago, when I moved to Oakland, I was set on this idea of like, I want the traditional

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:38.730
 American idea of home, like, I want a backyard and a picket fence. And like, I want to grow kale. And I

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:43.030
 want to know my neighbors. And I want to go to school board meetings. And it was a very romanticized

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:49.590
 idea of the definitive American dream home. And that fell apart within six months, like literally fell

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:56.680
 apart in every way that you can imagine. And I think now I've realized that because I don't belong

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.350
 in any of the places where I don't fully belong in Mumbai anymore. I didn't belong in Italy, where I

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:08.210
 went to high school. I definitely didn't belong in Southern California. And I don't belong in Oakland.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:14.850
 Home is going to have to be a transient thing. And it's going to have to be home is in seasons.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:22.510
 You know, Masin Hamid, I think is the author. He is an author from Lahore. And he wrote a couple

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:28.930
 incredible books, but he lives between New York and Lahore. And he wrote a New Yorker piece that I'll send

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:34.230
 you about severing and messing up every single romantic relationship he's ever had in the past decade,

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:40.310
 because every time he went to a place he would miss the other. And it would wreak complete havoc on his

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:48.390
 life. And he missed every sort of potential profitable real estate boom, because he could

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:52.490
 have bought property in New York, but he was thinking about Lahore. Then he could have brought property in

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:59.410
 Lahore and he was thinking about New York. And finally, he just realized that we are this very privileged

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:07.540
 bunch who have the ability to have home in seasons. Like right now for this next week, the Bay Area is

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:15.150
 home. And I feel very excited to have a home for a week. And then on June 20th, I'll go to Mumbai and

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:22.140
 that'll be home for six weeks. And then I will, I'm not sure where I'll be probably in the Philippines

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:31.140
 for all of August and that'll be home. And I think for the next few years, that's the healthiest mode

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:38.660
 for me because putting a lot of pressure on one of the homes has always let me down. Like the minute I

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:46.380
 put pressure on India being home, all the parts of India that don't understand me come glaring at me

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:54.000
 and sort of hurt and become very just hurtful. They feel like these places are rejecting me,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.780
 even though they're not, they're just not all encompassing. They're not places that are trying

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:03.700
 to give me grief. Does that kind of answer? Yeah. I think before we started recording,

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:09.090
 we were talking about how I can think of so many people whose lives are like that, right? And I think

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:16.110
 wherever you exist in the social strata is the, your place of clearest focus, right? So you can see

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:23.180
 others who look like you. So I say that for myself in the way that like, I see other artists who also

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:29.080
 do like social justice work throughout the world. And they're like, I could pick them out of a crowd

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:33.900
 in the airport or something, you know, it's like a funny, it's a funny thing, right? But I think before

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.870
 we were recording, I was sharing with you an experience of the campesinos I worked with in

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:43.890
 Southern Mexico, coming back and forth to the United States and them having memories and senses of

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:51.600
 belonging in both places and how I think food really offers one of those points of entry for

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:58.850
 people. Right. So does it for you? What has that been like for you? Um, I think that growing up Indian

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:06.640
 food, and I think a lot of immigrant kids have this story where your culture's food is hated on or made

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:12.910
 fun off or somehow not good. I grew up reading and in blatant books, which are this old cranky British

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:18.930
 lady who's apparently very homophobic. I know that now. Um, and I grew up reading about treacle pudding

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:26.990
 and like golden syrup and all these funny, a toad in a hole, egg in a hole, frog in a hole, that thing,

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:35.690
 toad in a hole, one of the three. Um, and that was my idea of good food. Me and my brother would save up

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:41.380
 all of our pocket money to go to the American store to buy fruit roll-ups. And that was the most

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:47.660
 aspirational food. All my aunts, when they came from America, they would bring Taco Bell salsa packets

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:54.560
 in like these large jars. Like they would bring maybe 200 Taco Bell salsa packets and we would eat

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:01.600
 them over the course of three months until another aunt came to visit. So I guess when I started trying

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:10.310
 to, when I left Mumbai to go to Italy, I was very confused about where I was. And I didn't understand

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:15.940
 that I was in, I was living in this village that was part of the Austro-Hungarian, used to be part of

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:21.440
 the Austro-Hungarian empire and was on the Slovenian-Italian border. And I didn't speak the language,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:27.160
 still just about speak the language. It's a dialect of Italian. So who speaks the language?

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:32.140
 And the only way I had to understand things was commerce, like was going to the supermarket and

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:36.200
 walking around and trying to understand why are people eating these biscuits? Or like,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:42.120
 how is it that it's culturally okay here to eat ice cream for dinner? Or, you know, I didn't grow up

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.340
 eating wheat three times a day and my digestive system is going to shit, but all of these people

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:50.910
 are managing it. It just became a way to ask questions and actually have them answered in a

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:56.470
 language I understood. Cause I just had to watch and eat and see. I went to stay with the host family and

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:03.820
 they, I guess, spending a week drinking wine with them at lunch helped me understand drinking culture

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:10.500
 in Italy in a way that had really confused me because in India, drinking is seen as a very sort

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:15.490
 of, drinking is associated with alcoholism. There's very little like middle ground of like, oh, casual

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:20.510
 drinking. Whereas here they were drinking at lunch. And I spent the first couple of days being worried

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:25.580
 that I was with these booze hounds. And then I was like, oh, it's just an Italian thing. And I think,

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:31.620
 so because I was 16 and it was at a time when I was asking questions about the world already and

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:36.440
 trying to understand the world, food just became an easy lens to answer all those questions. And then

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:41.960
 you also get to eat when you're asking those questions. So, you know, and then when I got to

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:47.880
 America, I thought that because I grew up with this very Americanized American influenced childhood

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:53.640
 that I was coming home. I was convinced that I was finally coming back to the promised land. It was

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:58.620
 the America that my parents had left for their, what I at the time thought was a very sad,

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:05.230
 non-American life. And I expected to get off the plane and just fit right in. And I knew all the music

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:11.540
 and all the TV shows. And that was not the case at all. I went through two years of straight up culture

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:17.220
 shock. Um, I thought I was entering a Hollywood set and instead I ended up in the Southern California

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:22.810
 desert, which is mostly cactus and white supremacists and strip malls. And I'd never

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:28.510
 been to a strip mall. Um, I didn't know why there was so much parking everywhere. Um, there was just a

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:35.070
 lot of things that shocked me. And again, the easiest way to try to understand it was through

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:41.440
 consumption. Um, not, I don't even want to say food. It was through, I spent hours walking around

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:46.270
 the grocery store. That was my go-to move to understand America. And then I also spent hours

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:50.810
 walking through the dining hall where I was like, wow, they eat peanut butter by the spoon here.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:58.050
 Um, just so many little things. Um, or that red meat was so big and you could eat so much of it.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:04.730
 I grew up mostly vegetarian and the idea of meat eating didn't make sense. And I would, I,

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:10.590
 I remember very clearly sitting in the dining hall as a freshman, watching the football players eat

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:17.540
 beef and just being amazed and just being like, that's like a pound of beef. Where's it going?

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:23.810
 I love grocery stores. I go to grocery stores everywhere in the world because for me, it's

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:29.390
 this very grounding experience of like understanding what people do. Right. And last week when I was

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:34.480
 talking to Nora, we were talking about markets in Mexico and what a central part of life that is.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:43.150
 But I think you learn so much by seeing how people buy food too. Right. Like I'm a shameless squeezer,

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:49.400
 like in the, in the produce section, I have to squeeze everything. Like it's really takes a long

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:54.300
 time for me to get through a produce section like that. And I also like have to talk to the guy and

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:58.340
 get, you know, know everything about it. It's really kind of uncomfortable for other people sometimes,

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:06.770
 but, but I think moving, that's such an interesting point of translation, right? To be going from one

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:13.410
 place to another. And this, I think this idea like that, you know, a place I have that experience so

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:18.300
 many times in my life of working internationally and living internationally where you have so much

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:22.720
 preconception about it. And then you show up and it's totally different, but food is something

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:33.380
 everybody's an expert in. You got to do it every day. And so how did, not only do you like eat too

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:39.000
 much, and that's, that's clearly, you know a lot about how you eat too much too, probably, but you

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.100
 started taking pictures of food and telling stories about food.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:49.760
 So when I was 15, I dropped out of high school, uh, before, right before 14, right before I went to

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:55.080
 Italy, I dropped out of school and, um, I'd gotten myself into large volumes of trouble,

00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:01.160
 was doing lots of drugs, hanging out with lots of boys. And we don't like boys anymore for that

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:07.550
 reason. Well, that and others. And my parents were incredible about it. They, I can't remember a

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:12.250
 single time that year that they shouted at me or questioned my general fuckery.

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:17.970
 I shouldn't swear. Um, but they, they were just like, okay, what do you want to do? What do you

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:22.670
 need? How do we make sure you get better? How do we make sure that you don't have to live this life

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:30.390
 or live like this? And my mom gave me her camera and she said, when I was young and angsty and

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:37.000
 really struggled, I like explored the city with the camera. And I think it was a time when Bombay

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:41.680
 really confused me. I didn't understand the city. I didn't understand my social position within it.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:47.620
 I was discovering what it meant to be an upper class Indian, but still be aware of the rest of the

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:52.560
 world. Because I think the way class works in India is that you ignore, you ignore your class.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:58.200
 You're very ignorant about it. And you put your blinders on. Um, and I didn't have blinders. I

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:03.660
 wasn't raised to have blinders. So it was a very uncomfortable experience of privilege and

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:09.010
 uncomfortable experience of becoming a teenager and being like, Oh shit. And one that my, my,

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:14.110
 the rest of my family didn't have like my little brother, I was waiting for him to turn 15 and go

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:18.790
 through the anxiety and angst that I did. And he breezed right through it. I think it made me realize

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:24.750
 that it was just something that was personal to me, but she gave me a camera and I started just

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:31.460
 walking around the city with her camera and it became only way I knew how to interact with the city.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:39.020
 I don't know how to converse with people. I still struggle with that because I feel very hyper aware

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:44.780
 of their positionality versus mine, but I know how to photograph the city and I know how to

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:52.180
 understand the history of the city through photography. So until I got to America, my,

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:59.340
 my predominant way of interacting with the world was with a camera. Um, and, and it really healed me.

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:06.880
 I think I went from being a really, really messed up kid who had, I mean, from drugs to sexual abuse to

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:14.210
 it all happened through my teenagehood. And that camera gave me confidence. There was things out

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:20.990
 there I could do in a place in the world. And so then once I got to the U S and was suddenly

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:27.150
 fascinated by consumption. And now that I'm saying it, that makes sense. America is about consumption

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:34.020
 and I'm fascinated by, by it. Um, it just became the most obvious thing to photograph. I it's also,

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:40.670
 it's not exactly a beautiful country. Um, yeah, I stand by that statement. It's not a beautiful

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:44.890
 country. I mean, if you go to national parks, it's a beautiful country, but often as an immigrant,

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:50.450
 national parks are not, um, how you experience the country. I mean, I was experiencing strip malls and

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:57.510
 they weren't quite as beautiful as, you know, you laid in Mumbai streets. So, so what I was then drawn

00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:04.190
 to was food to photograph. And I think I went through a pretty solid phase of being like, Ooh,

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:10.750
 food porn. I want to make food porn. And just finding it very exciting to document all my meals. Um,

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:20.550
 and that wore off within a couple of years where I think it was a, it was a professor who said she,

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:25.450
 she was very harsh where she said something along the lines of, do you really want to be that basic

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:36.360
 girl? And I was just like, wow. Um, thanks. And I don't, I don't think that's true. Um, but I think

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:44.160
 meeting PKC and meeting Sita, Sita, especially because she's an artist and thinks about visuals and

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:50.160
 representation a lot. And then also the podcast, the racist sandwich. Um, and I think they had,

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:55.240
 they had a podcast with somebody called a photographer called Celeste who really went

00:18:55.240 --> 00:19:01.380
 into how photography and visual representation has so much power. And especially if you're photographing

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:09.610
 food, the ability to change the narrative is huge. And I think that's where beyond just wanting to

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:16.570
 photograph food, it became, I see all these issues in the world of food and maybe my photos can change

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:21.530
 that because, because photos legitimize things, right? Like when there's a photo of something,

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:27.800
 it's real. And I don't think I'm there yet. I don't think I'm representing exactly what I want to be

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:32.880
 yet, but I think that's kind of what I'm working towards and what I, and what PKC has allowed me

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:40.700
 to do in a lot of ways there. My first gig. So your photographs are really beautiful. Like you

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:49.230
 like a pretty photo and what's that feel like? Um, Sita and I talked about this. I struggle with that,

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:57.380
 that I love beauty and I love prettiness. Um, I was raised by a family of designers. Um,

00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:04.050
 our home is beautiful. Aesthetics have just, I think from the day I was born, I was told that

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:11.460
 it is important that things are well-designed and gorgeous. Um, I think I've embraced that at this

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:17.560
 point that beautiful can be used to manipulate and then beautiful can also be used to tell difficult

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:23.770
 stories. An ex-girlfriend of mine actually said, oh yeah, I look at your Instagram mostly for the

00:20:23.770 --> 00:20:28.010
 pretty photos and I just ignore the rambles underneath because they're too emotional anyway.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:35.900
 Um, and I was like, well, there's a lot of people who like the emotion, dude. Um, yeah. Well,

