 a cake baker. Like those are not a thing to be. And yet I think that with the politics of

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:47.180
 exotification and, you know, like otherness of the world, like theoretically, like me as an American

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:52.970
 and you as an Indian, I should be asking you the story of like your spicy grandmother or something

00:41:52.970 --> 00:42:01.710
 right now, you know, and that I should be shocked when that's not your story. And I should really be

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:09.680
 arguing with you about how like you really know, but India really is like those. I mean, so you

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:14.800
 actually, they, they really are those like piles of spices like that, right? Like, you know, don't

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:22.130
 burst my bubble that way that there is this other place and there's purity and there's, um, something

00:42:22.130 --> 00:42:26.450
 that's untouched and, you know, all of these other things. And of course you're bursting

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:31.130
 someone's bubble by explaining Kleenex and that's amazing. That's such a good story. That one's

00:42:31.130 --> 00:42:36.240
 really one for the books, but why is it important that your work continues to do that? And that

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:42.200
 it continues to kind of, I don't know what's like, I think of Donna Haraway's description

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:45.940
 of like staying with the trouble, right? And following that.

00:42:47.470 --> 00:42:56.010
 I think because I found myself to be very colonized and very exoticizing for a long time where I felt

00:42:56.010 --> 00:43:07.880
 that a lot of me and my peers, fellow sort of Indian Brown, they see peers as self-hatred came from,

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:15.000
 um, not feeling real enough, feeling like, because we didn't know the spicy India, we were the bad

00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:22.170
 Indians or the half Indians or the misplaced Indians. And I think I know that unless I think about my

00:43:22.170 --> 00:43:31.880
 nieces a lot, I think about my nieces because they, this ties into other things. Um, I want

00:43:31.880 --> 00:43:39.560
 them to grow up, not questioning their identity at all. I guess if I didn't stay with the trouble,

00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:46.410
 I feel like I would have become or risk becoming because I have family members like this, shout

00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:51.250
 out, um, Trump supporting Indians who like to pretend that they're white and who are convinced

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:57.290
 that if they just like become doctors and work hard enough and buy enough in like three more

00:43:57.290 --> 00:44:02.490
 Audis, they will become white people in America. And they live, I think, I think with a pretty deep

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:07.080
 amount of shame about where they really come from and what their country really is. And eventually

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:14.350
 their idea of what their country really is changes to the expectation of it. And I think that I'm

00:44:14.350 --> 00:44:24.530
 terrified of being that and of losing the trouble. And I feel that I grew up in a country and a time

00:44:24.530 --> 00:44:33.830
 when tracing real answers of like, why do we like cricket so much? Why are we eating cutlets for

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:40.630
 dinner? Am I a real Indian? What does it mean to be real? How did the British influence my idea of

00:44:40.630 --> 00:44:48.670
 Indianness? How did the British influence the idea of India? Those questions were taboo and or not worth

00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:55.020
 answering. It was just like, keep your head down, do your shit. And it just left a lot of sort of blank

00:44:55.020 --> 00:45:01.390
 spaces and a lot of confusion. And I guess I feel like if I keep answering those questions or I keep

00:45:01.390 --> 00:45:06.680
 asking those questions, maybe it goes back to that, the idea of creating a new language. We'll create a new

00:45:06.680 --> 00:45:11.450
 language with which to speak. And then all the little girls that I love will be less angsty than I

00:45:11.450 --> 00:45:13.630
 was. I think that's the dream.

00:45:14.270 --> 00:45:19.560
 And then maybe that language is a question, right? Rather than an answer, right? Maybe

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:25.130
 these pictures are provocation rather than a statement.

00:45:25.950 --> 00:45:31.510
 I think right now they are provocation. I don't think I have enough of a statement. I think when

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:37.390
 I, somebody asked me the other day, you know, what does decolonization mean? And I said, dude,

00:45:37.510 --> 00:45:43.520
 that's a big question. But also right now, decolonization is a series of questions. And finding those

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:51.300
 answers and seeing where they take us. Because the problem with colonization and the post-colonialism

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:56.590
 is that nobody asked questions. We just pretended it didn't happen or erased it or squashed it.

00:45:58.030 --> 00:46:05.810
 And I think what's so interesting for me about that is decolonization is a question for me as a white

00:46:05.810 --> 00:46:14.700
 woman too, right? So I feel so enlivened by getting to be part of a conversation where there are these

00:46:14.700 --> 00:46:22.190
 provocations because it makes us all more visible to each other, right? I think the process of

00:46:22.190 --> 00:46:28.600
 colonization, there's so much guilt and shame in every direction about that. And it feels deeply

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:33.930
 humanizing to me to begin to contextualize things as they are. Not easy.

00:46:34.070 --> 00:46:35.670
 To complicate both sides.

00:46:36.310 --> 00:46:42.230
 Right. Exactly. Right. That the process of not knowing is a scary one for everybody on both sides.

00:46:42.530 --> 00:46:48.860
 And that I really, now I'm getting all sentimental about it, but I really, I really,

00:46:48.980 --> 00:46:55.820
 I love the inconclusiveness of that, right? I love the trouble that it sort of stirs up because

00:46:55.820 --> 00:47:02.360
 there are so many surprises in it. And for me in a world that feels so glum,

00:47:02.580 --> 00:47:11.400
 the hope is in the surprise, right? And so maybe, I think we're close to the end, but do you want to

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:15.340
 talk about whatever's next for you? The surprise?

00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:27.320
 The surprise is that I realized that spices have had a very complicated history in India for a very

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:34.390
 long time, like 600 years, kind of long time. And it really started when I was here and started seeing

00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:41.670
 the turmeric latte. And it really annoyed me. And I didn't know why it just stirred up so much anger

00:47:41.670 --> 00:47:49.610
 in me of, I guess, a lot of immigrants feel this about their food being so shameful their whole lives,

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:55.650
 and then it becoming cool. And just being like, wait, I had that all along. So there was this first

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:59.410
 emotional reaction that I quickly realized that there was very little I could do about, you know,

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:05.970
 move on. It's fine. Just don't drink the turmeric latte and don't pay dollars for it. But then when I

00:48:05.970 --> 00:48:11.180
 started looking into turmeric and the history of turmeric, it just seemed like there'd been this

00:48:11.180 --> 00:48:20.150
 history of exploitation that ran back all the way to the Portuguese and the history of white people in

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:28.300
 particular making money off of spices had gone on too long. And that system hasn't changed. Right now,

00:48:28.460 --> 00:48:36.260
 five spice companies export most of India's highest quality spices. And the government is in coats with

00:48:36.260 --> 00:48:42.980
 them. Like the government very much facilitates that. And the domestic market for spices is usually

00:48:42.980 --> 00:48:51.710
 corrupted with cornflour. Like most Indian spices are like 45% cornflour and then spice, whereas the West

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:57.210
 gets the really pure yummy stuff. And granted, it's because the West is willing to pay for it. Yes,

00:48:57.210 --> 00:49:06.150
 we get it. But it's also that was the system that that trade has gone on for so long that there was

00:49:06.150 --> 00:49:12.130
 never a correction of it of like, oh, maybe we deserve this too. So in February, when I quit my job,

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:18.370
 I said, well, if white women want to drink turmeric lattes, then I want to make sure that as many brown

00:49:18.370 --> 00:49:24.930
 farmers as possible can make money off of it, and as much money as possible off of it. And so that's

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:33.310
 what I'm doing. I'm working with four different cooperatives all over India. It started out as to

00:49:33.310 --> 00:49:40.240
 just research turmeric and see what they needed, what it looked like. And what I found was that continuous

00:49:40.240 --> 00:49:50.120
 cycle of a colonial narrative around food where in 1850s, some British stodgy dude decided that turmeric

00:49:50.120 --> 00:49:56.070
 from alopee was the best. Why? Because it was bright yellow. Great. Did it have the highest curcumin

00:49:56.070 --> 00:50:04.040
 content? Nope. Like, was it a particularly wonderful varietal for the soil? Did it grow very easily? Nope.

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:11.200
 Just bright yellow. And he was not a scientist. And so he didn't even look into the specific

00:50:11.200 --> 00:50:17.730
 varietal. He was just like, all the bright yellow ones, those are alopee turmeric. And even now at a

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:24.440
 spice shop, what you want, the most sought after turmeric is alopee turmeric. But scientifically,

00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:33.390
 it doesn't exist. There's no such thing as AFT, alopee finger turmeric. There's pratiba turmeric,

00:50:33.390 --> 00:50:39.240
 which is this beautiful high curcumin content varietal that's really healthy for you and grows

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:46.230
 really, really well with cacao. There's madhvi. There's an incredible wealth of turmeric varietals.

00:50:46.370 --> 00:50:52.340
 There's purple turmeric. There's mango flavored turmeric. There's so much turmeric. But because

00:50:52.340 --> 00:50:59.500
 we are still following this colonial idea of what turmeric is, and what it should look like,

00:51:00.020 --> 00:51:06.840
 farmers, they don't have the market for the real product and the true Indian exotic product.

00:51:07.390 --> 00:51:16.660
 So I guess my plan for this cooperative and this business is to slowly tell deeper stories,

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:23.950
 but through selling spice. And I think that these farmers have been, quote unquote,

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:30.550
 fucked over too many times to do it in a fast sort of buy tons of turmeric from them, process it and

00:51:30.550 --> 00:51:35.200
 export it here. In all of my conversations with them, they've made it very clear that they would

00:51:35.200 --> 00:51:43.180
 want it to be a very slow, high investment process. So right now, what I have to invest is a lot of time

00:51:43.180 --> 00:51:48.830
 and a lot of big eyes and big questions. That's what I'm investing. And hopefully over the next five

00:51:48.830 --> 00:51:51.470
 years, it's a lot more than that. So that's the plan.

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:58.150
 That's so exciting. Well, great. And so how can we follow along with you?

00:51:58.490 --> 00:52:02.070
 You can find me at my sometimes updated website,

00:52:02.430 --> 00:52:08.880
 sanajavarikadri.com or on Instagram, which is honestly where I am most days of the week,

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:18.890
 which is sanajavarikadri, S-A-N-A-J-A-V-E-R-I-K-E-D-R-I or in Mumbai. Yeah. Come visit any of you.

00:52:19.270 --> 00:52:24.690
 Perfect. Well, thanks so much for being here today. Yeah. Thank you. I feel like I was given

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:29.210
 language to think about things and talk about things through this conversation, which is really nice.

